4,Umbundu — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/Umbundu,"January 17, 2026 - Umbundu, also known as South Mbundu or Úmbúndú, is a Bantu language belonging to the Niger-Congo family and serving as the primary tongue of the Ovimbundu ethnic group in central Angola.[1][2] It is the northernmost Southern Bantu language and features a tonal system with three tones (high, low, and downstepped high), alongside a Latin-based orthography adapted for its phonology.[2][1][3] With approximately 23% of Angola's population speaking Umbundu, or about 9 million people (2023 est.), it is the most widely spoken indigenous language in the country after Portuguese.[4][5] The Ovimbundu,",Umbundu,Niger-Congo
